    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Children with disabilities in Azerbaijan experience educational isolation with the majority segregated into educational programs such as homeschooling and special education. The educational isolation of students with disabilities underscores the national education system’s inability to provide inclusive learning opportunities for these students. Through qualitative analysis of policy documents and interviews with key stakeholders—including a national policymaker, an advocate of international principles, a practitioner, and a scholar—this study presents the first empirical analysis of Azerbaijan’s inclusive education policy in relation to international frameworks, with a specific focus on the internalization of global norms within national education policies and practices. Drawing on neo-institutionalist theory, the paper examines the discrepancies between international and national inclusive education models and the possible reasons for gaps between policy and practice. These discussions pave the way for the development of stronger inclusive education opportunities for children with disabilities in Azerbaijan.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Omarova, N. (2024). Push and Pull Dynamics: A Comparative Perspective of Azerbaijan’s Inclusive Education Policy. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/dg517rt0002. https://doi.org/10.25740/dg517rt0002.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
